I have a value that may have multiple types at runtime, but in practice it always has one of those types. How can I tell the type system this?

For example, if I have a function that takes a callback, and that callback may be of any type, I can't call it like this:

callback()
because the compiler will complain that callback is of type unknown. I could call it like this:

(callback as any)()
but that would defeat the purpose of TypeScript.

Is there a way to tell the type system "I know that in theory this value may have these multiple types, but in practice I know it will only have this one"? For example, in a language like Haskell, I could write this:

foo :: (a, b) -> c
foo (x, y) =...

but in TypeScript, I can't.

The only thing that comes to mind is to write a type guard that checks that the value is of a certain type, but that's not ideal. For example:

function isNumber(value: unknown): value is number {
return typeof value === "number";
}

function foo(callback: unknown) {
if (isNumber(callback)) {
// do something with callback as number
} else if (isString(callback)) {
// do something with callback as string
} else {
// do something with callback as unknown
}
}
