A common subsequence is a sequence that appears in the same relative order, but not necessarily contiguous, in each of the given sequences. For example, the longest common subsequence of "ABCDGH" and "AEDFHR" is "ADH" of length 3. The longest common subsequence can be computed in O(n2) time and O(n) space, where n is the length of the given sequences.

Here is the code for the function:
```def lcs(X, Y, m, n):
    L = [[None]*(n+1) for i in range(m+1)]
    for i in range(m+1):
        for j in range(n+1):
            if i == 0 or j == 0:
                L[i][j] = 0
            elif X[i-1] == Y[j-1]:
                L[i][j] = L[i-1][j-1] + 1
            else:
                L[i][j] = max(L[i-1][j], L[i][j-1])
    return L[m][n]
```
The code uses dynamic programming to compute the length of the longest common subsequence between two input strings X and Y of lengths m and n respectively. It initializes a 2D array L of size (m+1)x(n+1) and fills it with zeros. Then, it iterates over each element of the array and checks whether the current characters at positions i and j in X and Y respectively are equal. If they are, it updates the value of L[i][j] as the sum of L[i-1][j-1] and 1. Otherwise, it updates the value of L[i][j] as the maximum of L[i-1][j] and L[i][j-1]. Finally, it returns the value stored at L[m][n], which represents the length of the longest common subsequence between X and Y.
